'''James Key''' (born 14 January 1972) is a British engineer who has worked in [[Formula One]]. He is currently the technical director of  [[Sauber Motorsport|Stake F1 Team Kick Sauber]].

==Education==
James Key studied Mechanical Engineering at the University of Nottingham. Lotus Engineering sponsored him to his degree in 1996.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nottingham.ac.uk/connectonline/features/2017/mclaren/the-will-to-win-careers-advice-to-get-ahead.aspx|title=The will to win - careers advice to get ahead - University of Nottingham - The University of Nottingham|website=www.nottingham.ac.uk|access-date=2020-01-26|archive-date=26 January 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200126181513/https://www.nottingham.ac.uk/connectonline/features/2017/mclaren/the-will-to-win-careers-advice-to-get-ahead.aspx|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>[https://www.nottingham.ac.uk/businessalumni/news/universities-and-formula-one-share-high-performance-culture.aspx „Alumni James Key (Mechanical Engineering, 1996)“]</ref>

==Formula One career==
Key joined [[Jordan Grand Prix]] in 1998 spending several years as a data engineer, then became [[race engineer]] for [[Takuma Sato]]. Following a year in the wind tunnel he transferred to the vehicle dynamics department, eventually becoming the department head during the team's final few seasons as Jordan Grand Prix.

Shortly after the team's ownership transferred to [[Midland F1 Racing|MF1 Racing]], he became Technical Director during the [[2005 Formula One season]] following a brief period as Technical Co-ordinator. He was one of the youngest Technical Directors of a Formula One team, at the age of 33 years, along with [[Sam Michael]] (born in 1971) who became the technical director of the [[WilliamsF1|Williams]] F1 team at the age of 33 during the [[2004 Formula One season|2004 season]]. Key retained his position during the team's transition through [[Spyker F1]] to [[Force India F1]]. 

In April 2010 he left Force India to join the [[Sauber]] team, replacing [[Willy Rampf]] as Technical Director.<ref name=":0">{{cite news|last=Beer|first=Matt|title=Key takes over as Sauber technical boss|publisher=[[Haymarket Group|Haymarket Publications]]|work=autosport.com|url=http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/81675|date=24 February 2010|access-date=24 February 2010}}</ref> He remained there for almost two years, before leaving in February 2012 to accept an undisclosed offer with one of the British-based teams.<ref>{{cite news|last=Noble|first=Jonathan|title=Technical director James Key to part company with Sauber|publisher=[[Haymarket Group|Haymarket Publications]]|work=autosport.com|url=http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/97308|date=3 February 2012|access-date=3 February 2012}}</ref>

On 6 September 2012, it was announced that Key had joined [[Scuderia Toro Rosso]] as Technical Director, replacing Giorgio Ascanelli.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.formula1.com/news/headlines/2012/9/13751.html|title=Key replaces Ascanelli at Toro Rosso|date=6 September 2012|website=www.formula1.com|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130929123710/http://www.formula1.com/news/headlines/2012/9/13751.html|archive-date=29 September 2013|access-date=19 May 2019}}</ref>

On 26 July 2018, [[McLaren]] confirmed that Key had agreed to become technical director of the team, replacing the ousted [[Tim Goss]]. On 22 February 2019, it was announced that Key would join McLaren from 25 March 2019, just after the Australian Grand Prix.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Beer |first1=Matt |title=Toro Rosso: Key to join McLaren as F1 technical boss after Melbourne |url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/141698/key-to-join-mclaren-after-2019-melbourne-race}}</ref> He formed a triumvirate with [[Andrea Stella (engineer)|Andrea Stella]] as Racing Director and Piers Thynne as Production Director, all under Team Principal [[Andreas Seidl]].<ref>{{Cite news|title=Stella promoted in McLaren restructure|language=en-GB|work=BBC Sport|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/50977329|access-date=2020-07-25}}</ref> Key was sacked by McLaren on 23 March 2023 in an organisational change of the team's executive technical director role, replaced by [[David Sanchez (engineer)|David Sanchez]] following team dissatisfaction with the initial design and early season performance of their 2023 challenger - the [[McLaren MCL60|MCL60]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Noble |first=Jonathan |date=2023-03-23 |title=Key out, Sanchez] in as McLaren restructures F1 team |language=en-GB |work=Motorsport.com |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/key-out-sanchez-in-as-mclaren-restructures-f1-team/10447549/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=McLaren Formula 1 team announces organisational changes |url=https://www.mclaren.com/racing/formula-1/2023/mclaren-formula-1-team-announces-organisational-changes/ |access-date=2023-03-23 |website=www.mclaren.com |language=en-GB}}</ref>

On 7 June 2023, [[Alfa Romeo in Formula One|Alfa Romeo F1 Team Stake]] announced that Key would join the team on 1 September 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |title=James Key joins Alfa Romeo F1 Team Stake as Technical Director |url=https://www.sauber-group.com/motorsport/f1-news/james-key-joins-alfa-romeo-f1-team-stake-as-technical-director/ |access-date=2023-06-07 |website=www.sauber-group.com |language=en-GB}}</ref> He took on the role of Technical Director again, replacing the ousted [[Jan Monchaux]]. He previously held the position of Technical Director under this team under its former name of Sauber in 2013, ahead of their re-branding as the [[Audi]] works team in 2026.<ref name=":0" /> He once again worked under [[Andreas Seidl]], CEO of Sauber Group, who had served as Team Principal during the pair's time at McLaren. Seidl was later replaced at Sauber Group by [[Mattia Binotto]].

